---
.title = "iroh: encrypted peer-to-peer",
.date = @date("2026-06-17T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

iroh is pancake's encrypted transport, and the one that lets two peers reach each
other without a server in the middle. A session's bytes ride
[QUIC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html) carried by
[iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/), and a peer is named by a self-authenticating
key rather than a `host:port`. Everything above the wire stays the same OCapN
pancake speaks over TCP, so the only thing that changes when you switch to iroh is
how a peer is addressed and the fact that the channel is now encrypted and
authenticated end to end.

## One key does three jobs

The piece worth understanding first is what an iroh peer's identity *is*. Each
endpoint is an Ed25519 keypair, and pancake makes that single key carry three
roles at once:

- it is the **iroh node id** the transport authenticates the QUIC channel against;
- it is the **OCapN locator designator**, the name you dial in an
  `ocapn://<node-id>.iroh` URI;
- it is the **session signing key** that signs the `op:start-session` handshake.

Because one key fills all three roles, the peer you cryptographically connected to,
the name you dialed, and the identity that signed the session are provably the same
party. `zig build iroh-interop` checks that knot on a live wire from both ends: the
partner's verified session pubkey equals the node id the transport authenticated
equals the signed locator designator. There is no separate certificate to trust and
no address to spoof, because the address *is* the key.

That identity also outlives the process that minted it. An endpoint bound from the
same seed comes back with the same node id at a fresh network address, so a
sturdyref — a node id plus a swiss-number — still names the same capability after a
restart. The interop gate proves that too, by tearing an endpoint down and reaching
the same published cap through the locator recorded before the restart.

## Reaching a peer

A peer hands you its node id; how that id turns into a route is a *reachability
posture* you choose when you bind the endpoint. pancake exposes four:

| Posture | How a dial resolves | Proven by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `direct` | the dialer carries the peer's direct socket addresses as locator hints | `zig build iroh-interop` |
| `local_discovery` | an in-process address book resolves the id, no relay, fully deterministic | `zig build iroh-interop` |
| `discovery` | the public number-0 relays plus DNS/pkarr resolve the id over the open internet | `zig build iroh-reachability` |
| `discovery_no_relay` | DNS/pkarr resolve the id, but with relay fallback disabled — published direct addresses only | `zig build bindings-iroh-redeem-posture` |

`direct` and `local_discovery` are deterministic and run as the standard interop
gate. `discovery` reaches real n0 infrastructure: a server publishes its node info
to pkarr on bind, and a client dials by the 64-hex id alone with an empty hint set,
so a session that opens proves the route was discovered, not hinted. Because it
depends on public infrastructure and propagation timing, it is opt-in and never part
of the hermetic `zig build check`. `discovery_no_relay` is the same discovery path
with relay fallback disabled; that a hint-less give is governed by the configured
posture is proven hermetically by `zig build bindings-iroh-redeem-posture`.

## A separate artifact, by design

iroh's QUIC stack is a Rust library, so it rides a Rust sidecar staticlib that
pancake keeps in its own compiled artifact, `libpancake_net_transport_iroh`
([ADR-0055](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0055-iroh-netlayer-sidecar.md)).
The base archives stay pure Zig and dependency-free; a program links iroh only when
it wants it. `zig build iroh-abi` builds that companion archive and its header
(it needs `cargo`).

The same split shows up in the bindings. Each one keeps iroh in a per-transport
module — the `@pancake/iroh` package, `pancake.iroh`, `pancake-iroh.rkt`, and Rust's opt-in
`iroh` feature — and `connect` loads it lazily the first time it sees an `iroh`
locator. A program that only ever speaks TCP never loads the iroh artifact. Hosting
over iroh adds one verb, `listen_iroh`; dialing a peer over iroh goes through the
same `connect` as TCP, and the only difference a caller sees is the address.

## Scale: many connections, one thread

The shipped iroh resident serves one connection per OS thread, the
one-thread-per-connection path that `iroh-interop` proves correct. For the fan-out pancake targets
elsewhere, that caps too early, so iroh also has an event-driven path
([ADR-0057](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0057-iroh-readiness-loop.md)):
a loop host registers the sidecar's single readiness descriptor once and drives
non-blocking frame I/O across every connection from one thread. `zig build iroh-loop`
proves it by dialing many endpoints at the server at once and checking that every
call on every connection returns correct, so the one loop thread is provably
multiplexing them rather than serving them in turn. The Python, Rust, and
Racket bindings prove the same shape over the C ABI through `python-iroh-loop`,
`rust-iroh-loop`, and `racket-iroh-loop` (or `bindings-iroh-loop` for all
three).

What the encrypted channel costs per call is measured too: the
[Performance]($link.page('performance')) page times a full OCapN round-trip over iroh
beside a cleartext TCP baseline on one machine, so the price of QUIC and encryption is
a number you can read rather than a guess. The per-connection memory this path adds
lives in the Rust sidecar's heap, separate from the sans-io core that page also
measures.

## Three-party handoff over iroh

A capability you receive from one peer can name an object that lives on a third, and
the handoff lets you reach that third party directly. Over iroh that means the
recipient dials the introduced exporter by its node id and opens a fresh encrypted
session to it. `zig build iroh-mesh` proves the full three-party handoff across real
iroh endpoints, so the introduction survives the jump from one encrypted channel to
another.

## See it run

Every claim here is a runnable gate (each needs `cargo`, and the live discovery one
needs the public internet):

```sh
zig build iroh-interop       # keyed conversation, dial-by-id, key unification, durable identity
zig build iroh-reachability  # dial-by-id over n0 relays + DNS/pkarr (opt-in, public internet)
zig build iroh-loop          # many concurrent connections served from one readiness loop
zig build iroh-mesh          # three-party handoff reached over iroh by node id
zig build iroh-abi           # build libpancake_net_transport_iroh and its header
```

For a full application over the encrypted channel, the [rooms]($link.page('examples'))
chat runs across five languages over iroh with `zig build demo-rooms-iroh`. The
[Transports]($link.page('transports')) page sets iroh beside the TCP and in-process
residents, and [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains locators, hints, and
sturdyrefs in general.
